/*
 * DEBUG ONLY - why did the RCU grace-period kthread starve? (v3)
 *
 * On the single-core DVF101 (HZ=100, PREEMPT) the SCHED_NORMAL rcu_preempt
 * kthread got no useful CPU for 21 s and panic_on_rcu_stall fired.  v1 found
 * the CPU owner: the SCHED_RR ms_selectThread looping on poll(), recvfrom()
 * -EAGAIN and usleep(5).  The analysis then pointed at a UDP socket whose
 * error queue still held an ICMP error while sk_err had already been taken:
 * poll() keeps reporting POLLERR and recv() keeps returning -EAGAIN.  This
 * file records what is needed to prove that chain, or show what else
 * happened, in one crash log:
 *
 *  - CPU history (starve_dbg_switch() + tick): per 100 ms the run time of the
 *    top tasks, RT/CFS/idle shares, RCU GP kthread run time, context
 *    switches and hard IRQs.  The last 64 s are kept.
 *  - Starvation episodes (starve_dbg_tick()): the GP kthread showed no
 *    activity for thresh_ms while a grace period is pending.  Episodes less
 *    than 10 s apart form one stall.  During a stall: tick samples (PCs),
 *    CFS slice and RT run length histograms, IRQ deltas.
 *  - Syscall profile of the RT user tasks during a stall
 *    (starve_dbg_sys_enter/exit(), forced with TIF_SYSCALL_TRACEPOINT):
 *    count, return values, duration, user call site, fd -> file/socket.
 *  - UDP sockets with IP_RECVERR (net hooks): error queue enqueue/dequeue,
 *    every consumption of sk_err (recv/send/SO_ERROR) with task and user
 *    call site, and "stuck" periods: POLLERR or -EAGAIN while sk_err == 0
 *    and the error queue is not empty.
 *
 * Output, all lines start with "STARVE:", summary first:
 *  - "early" report when an episode starts (at most every early_interval_s);
 *  - one line per episode end, stall end and socket stuck for 1 s;
 *  - full report from the RCU stall code right before panic_on_rcu_stall();
 *  - manual full report: echo 1 > /sys/module/starve_dbg/parameters/dump
 * Only the summary and the frame lines use KERN_ERR (slow serial console);
 * the rest is KERN_INFO and goes to the log buffer / pstore only.
 *
 * User addresses are printed as <file>+0x<file offset>.  For ms the text
 * segment starts at file offset 0, so "addr2line -f -e ms 0x<offset>" works
 * directly.  "in A <- B" is the innermost APCS frame of the user stack:
 * A lies in the calling ms function, B is where that function was called.
 *
 * State is only changed with interrupts disabled; this is a UP system.
